@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+# Local Catalyst receipt integration
+
+This is a local integration exercise, not an official research submission.
+The accompanying check uses this document as its submission note so the actual
+Yukon CLI follows its normal note and attribution requirements. No optimization
+claim is made. The baseline harness used for the initial live check retained
+the pinned ECDSA implementation, used zero model credits, and was independently
+verified by Catalyst before this Yukon integration test.
+
+The current provider implements the project-independent
+[`yukon-remote-verification-v1` contract](remote-verification.md). It posts to
+`/yukon/v1/retrieve`; only Catalyst interprets the receipt's internal fields.
+The original live VM test below used the older `catalyst-yukon-v2` endpoint.
+The VM has not been upgraded in this contract change. Before using the current
+checkout against that VM, update the Catalyst service to provide the generic
+endpoint. No new harness execution is needed; the existing receipt remains valid.
+
+## Components and ownership
+
+Catalyst and Yukon are separate services. Catalyst accepts the harness source,
+builds it, runs it through Dungeness, independently verifies the resulting
+candidate, and retains source, configuration, artifacts, accounting and scores.
+It returns a verification receipt referring to that completed evaluation.
+
+Yukon receives only this receipt. Its CLI sends the receipt through the ordinary
+submission upload endpoint. The API authenticates the local solver, stores the
+upload and queues validation in PostgreSQL. The real Yukon worker claims that
+job and invokes RemoteVerificationExecutionProvider. The provider calls the
+configured Catalyst service, carrying the receipt, the account ID from Yukon's
+stored submission record, the benchmark and submission IDs, and the requested
+Pareto policies and fields. Scores supplied by a solver are not authoritative.
+
+Catalyst checks the owner and exact receipt against its retained evaluation.
+The receipt binds the evaluation ID, source digest, configuration, problem,
+pricing, policies and image identity. The worker parses the returned judgment
+using Yukon's existing Pareto parser and records it through the normal store.
+The test reads the completed scores from Yukon's HTTP API, after persistence.
+This is different from calling the provider directly: API authentication, CLI
+packaging, archive storage, the durable queue, worker dispatch and PostgreSQL
+result storage all participate.
+
+## Start the local API and worker
+
+Install the locked dependencies in this Yukon checkout:
+
+```sh
+cd /Users/bxue/Documents/yukon/yukon
+bun install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts
+```
+
+Keep a private connection to the existing Catalyst VM in one terminal. If you
+already have this exact tunnel running, reuse it:
+
+```sh
+ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \
+  -L 127.0.0.1:8878:127.0.0.1:8778 openclaw
+```
+
+In a second terminal, start the local Yukon service:
+
+```sh
+cd /Users/bxue/Documents/yukon/yukon
+bun scripts/catalyst-local-api.ts \
+  /Users/bxue/Documents/catalyst/.catalyst/yukon-local \
+  /Users/bxue/Documents/catalyst/.catalyst/remote-vm/yukon-receipt-workspace \
+  /Users/bxue/Documents/catalyst/.catalyst/remote-vm/yukon-verifiers.json
+```
+
+The arguments are a private state directory, a workspace containing benchmark.json
+and a completed remote verification receipt, and a private verifier configuration.
+Paths above are the prepared local environment; another operator supplies their
+own paths and provisioned identities. No credential appears in the command.
+The consumer configuration maps the manifest's service name to a URL, consumer
+token and baseline owner ID. It must point to a service supporting
+`yukon-remote-verification-v1`. Plain HTTP is permitted only for the loopback SSH tunnel.
+The private service entry also needs `benchmarks[benchmarkId]` with
+`enabled: true` and the expected `problem: {id, version}`. The Catalyst consumer
+key needs the same scoped grant. See [the shared contract](remote-verification.md).
+The launcher never creates these permissions. On a fresh import it reports the
+assigned benchmark UUID and stops with the baseline queued, before starting any
+worker. Configure both operator grants and restart to continue.
+
+The launcher creates a dedicated local PostgreSQL Docker container for this
+state directory, with a port bound to 127.0.0.1. It never accepts a production
+database URL and never clears existing tables. It applies the repository's real
+database migrations. PostgreSQL remains available after Ctrl+C so records can
+be inspected and survive a server restart. The container name and port are
+recorded in the private state directory's database.json.
+
+The launcher provisions a local test account whose UUID matches the configured
+Catalyst owner. This is explicit local test provisioning, not hosted login or
+an impersonation feature exposed by the production server. A private local API
+key and CLI config are written under the state directory. The user's normal
+Yukon login and hosted account are not modified.
+
+The launcher imports Catalyst through the real local Yukon HTTP endpoint and
+lets the worker retrieve the baseline judgment from Catalyst. It then opens
+that local benchmark and prints READY. The benchmark UUID belongs only to this
+local Yukon database. The launcher starts the API and worker together; there is
+no separate manual worker command and it does not submit a solver receipt.
+
+## Submit separately through the actual CLI
+
+After READY, run this in another terminal:
+
+```sh
+cd /Users/bxue/Documents/yukon/yukon
+bun scripts/check-catalyst-local.ts \
+  /Users/bxue/Documents/catalyst/.catalyst/yukon-local
+```
+
+This check runs the actual Yukon CLI `submit` command with the isolated local
+configuration and the prepared receipt. The child environment excludes hosted
+API tokens and disables telemetry and trace synchronization. The result is
+polled from the local Yukon HTTP API and saved to last-result.json. The output
+includes the Yukon submission and job IDs, the Pareto tuple, validity, and the
+stored evaluator/commit provenance.
+
+To use the CLI manually, consult connection.json for the local benchmark ID,
+API URL, client workspace and configuration directory. Set YUKON_CONFIG_DIR to
+that private configuration directory, unset inherited YUKON_API_TOKEN,
+HILBERT_API_TOKEN and SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN, set YUKON_API_URL to the local API,
+and run the checkout's apps/cli/src/yukon.ts from the client workspace. Supply
+the benchmark ID, --model, --harness and a real --note-file as usual. The check
+script provides the same isolated invocation automatically.
+
+Only the configured receipt file is allowed in a submission archive, capped at
+64 KiB. Source files, evaluation settings, credentials, symlinks and additional
+archive entries are refused. Changing a local harness after evaluation cannot
+change what a receipt denotes. A new harness evaluation produces its own
+receipt; the existing receipt continues to refer to the retained source.
+
+## Evidence and limits of the test
+
+The remote harness evaluation is reused. No setup or benchmark command executes
+in the Yukon retrieval provider, and this test cannot enqueue Catalyst work.
+The provisioned consumer credential can retrieve verified judgments but cannot
+start evaluations or download private traces. Retrying a retrieval is safe.
+Submitting the same archive again is deduplicated by Yukon's normal API/store
+logic, and does not create a new validation job or another model run.
+
+The local repository adapter replaces GitHub network publication with Git
+objects in a private local repository under the state directory. It uses the
+same validated receipt archive and submission-tree builder. Actual commit SHAs
+are created locally so Yukon's normal Pareto evidence requirements are tested.
+These are test records, not commits or pushes to the user's source checkout.
+The first solver may submit the same receipt used as the baseline: receipt
+submissions must therefore support a commit whose tree equals the baseline.
+Ordinary source submissions continue to require code changes.
+
+The API, worker, store, archive validation and remote scoring provider are real
+Yukon components. The private receipt service supplies the real stored result.
+The local account bootstrap and local Git adapter are development plumbing.
+This test does not exercise hosted Yukon login, GitHub App permissions, hosted
+network connectivity, public registration, a hosted leaderboard, billing or
+provider credential renewal. Those remain release and deployment work.
+
+Stop the local API and worker with Ctrl+C. To stop the dedicated database,
+read its container name from database.json and run `docker stop` on that exact
+container. Do not remove the container if you want to retain the local records.
+Restarting the launcher reuses the state, account, benchmark and stored results.
+Closing the private SSH tunnel stops connectivity to Catalyst but does not stop
+the Catalyst VM service or erase any evaluations.
