@@ -0,0 +1,209 @@
+# Remote verification contract v1
+
+Yukon can publish results that another service has already verified. A solver
+sends source or other work to that service, receives a public receipt, and
+submits only the receipt to Yukon. Yukon's worker retrieves the authoritative
+judgment using a private service credential. Retrieval never starts execution
+or incurs model usage. Services do not need to be Catalyst or run harnesses.
+
+The normative wire schemas live in
+`src/execution/remote-verification-contract.ts`; the JSON Schema export is
+`docs/remote-verification-v1.schema.json`. The protocol is
+`yukon-remote-verification-v1`. Version 1 supports manual Pareto benchmarks.
+Scalar scoring and automatic source promotion are outside this version.
+
+## Registration and trust
+
+A pinned benchmark manifest selects a service alias and a receipt path:
+
+```json
+{
+  "provider": "remote-verification",
+  "service": "compression-lab",
+  "receiptPath": "submission/verification.json"
+}
+```
+
+The Yukon operator configures each approved alias in the private JSON file
+selected by `YUKON_REMOTE_VERIFIERS_FILE`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "compression-lab": {
+    "url": "https://verifier.example",
+    "token": "<private service credential>",
+    "baselineOwnerId": "<provisioned Yukon account UUID>",
+    "benchmarks": {
+      "22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222": {
+        "enabled": true,
+        "problem": {"id": "compression", "version": "dataset-sha256:1234"}
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The manifest is not an authorization. Before any retrieval request, Yukon
+requires an operator-controlled entry enabling this exact benchmark under this
+service alias. Missing entries, a missing `benchmarks` map, and `enabled: false`
+all deny retrieval. The problem binding comes from this entry, never the receipt
+or an editable manifest. The flag applies to baseline and submission retrieval.
+Changes require reloading/restarting the worker; configuration is read at startup.
+
+Use a separate scoped service token for each integration. The verifier must
+authenticate Yukon, independently authorize that consumer for the benchmark and
+expected problem/version, map the account UUID to an authorized evaluation owner,
+and compare the evaluated problem/version in its **retained run metadata** with
+the authorized problem binding. Also check supported policies/objectives. A submitted owner
+claim is never authorization. Yukon sends the account ID from its stored,
+authenticated submission record; baseline retrieval uses the operator's
+configured owner. How a verifier provisions those identities is service-specific.
+
+The alias, endpoint and credential are operator decisions. Receipts cannot
+choose a destination, redirect a request or supply credentials. HTTPS is
+required; loopback HTTP supports a private tunnel or local test. Redirects
+are rejected. Receipt archives contain exactly one regular JSON file, at most
+64 KiB. Its object contents are opaque to Yukon. They must be public-safe:
+receipts are retained in submission Git history, and judgment metrics are
+published as evaluation evidence.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+Send `POST /yukon/v1/retrieve` under the configured base URL, with
+`Authorization: Bearer <service token>` and `Content-Type: application/json`.
+
+```json
+{
+  "protocol": "yukon-remote-verification-v1",
+  "kind": "submission",
+  "owner_id": "11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111",
+  "benchmark_id": "22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222",
+  "benchmark_revision": "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa",
+  "submission_id": "44444444-4444-4444-8444-444444444444",
+  "problem": {"id": "compression", "version": "dataset-sha256:1234"},
+  "reference": {"experiment": "compression/run:17", "artifact": {"revision": "sha256:abc"}},
+  "objectives": [
+    {"name": "compressedBytes", "direction": "-"},
+    {"name": "decodeSeconds", "direction": "-"}
+  ],
+  "judge": {
+    "policies": {"scoring": {"version": "compression-v1"}, "validity": {"version": "lossless-v1"}},
+    "fields": {"scores": true, "validity": true}
+  }
+}
+```
+
+All listed keys are required. No extra top-level keys are allowed. The receipt
+is an arbitrary JSON object; policy values are arbitrary JSON owned by the
+benchmark. `problem.id` identifies the logical task; `problem.version` identifies
+an immutable dataset or task-contract revision, preferably a content digest.
+The verifier defines that namespace and verifies it from stored execution
+evidence. It must not just echo the requested problem or trust receipt labels.
+`benchmark_revision` separately identifies the pinned Yukon benchmark source.
+The ordered objective names and directions come from that same manifest.
+At least one judgment field must be requested. Scores follow objective order.
+Unknown versions, policies or objectives must fail closed.
+
+For `kind: "baseline"`, there is no solver submission. `submission_id` equals
+`benchmark_id`, and the receipt comes from the pinned benchmark repository.
+It is still a read of completed evidence. Registration never launches work.
+
+A successful response is HTTP 200:
+
+```json
+{
+  "protocol": "yukon-remote-verification-v1",
+  "request": "<the complete request object, echoed exactly as JSON>",
+  "artifact": {"kind": "solution-source", "sha256": "bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb"},
+  "judgment": {
+    "scores": [120, 4],
+    "valid": true,
+    "reason": "Lossless output independently verified.",
+    "metrics": {"experiment": "compression/run:17"}
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`request` is an object; the string above abbreviates it. Object key order does
+not matter. Yukon checks equality of every request field, validates the
+judgment using its existing Pareto parser, and retains the requested fields.
+`artifact` identifies the actual verified submission: the service defines its
+kind and canonical hashing scheme and returns a lowercase SHA-256 digest computed
+from the retained artifact. For Catalyst, it is the canonical harness source
+manifest digest with kind `harness-source`. A solver's descriptive harness name
+is not this identity. Yukon stores `{service, problem, artifact}` in the reserved
+`judgment.metrics.remoteVerification` namespace alongside the score, preserving
+other public service metrics. A service-provided value in that reserved namespace
+cannot overwrite the checked binding.
+
+A minimal receipt may be `{"token":"<opaque random lookup ID>"}`. The verifier
+resolves it to an immutable record containing owner, problem/version, artifact
+digest, policy and judgment. A token is a reference, not a credential or ownership
+proof. Copying it must not confer permission to claim another owner's result.
+An authenticated lookup needs no separately signed token. Signatures become
+necessary only for a future design accepting evidence without consulting the service.
+When scores are requested, a measurable result has exactly one finite number
+per objective. Tuple elements cannot be null or missing. The entire score tuple
+may be null for an unscored result, following Yukon's existing Pareto rules.
+When validity is requested, `valid` is required and invalid results require a
+nonempty reason. `metrics` is optional public JSON, never private traces,
+credentials, source archives or account details. The service must return only
+public result data. Scores claimed inside a receipt are not authoritative.
+
+Yukon caps responses at 4 MiB, uses a 30-second request timeout, and honors
+submission cancellation. This is a completed-result interface, not a polling
+or execution API. A service must resolve its own receipt against immutable
+stored evidence before responding.
+
+## Retries and errors
+
+The service atomically binds `(authenticated consumer, benchmark_id,
+submission_id)` to the receipt, owner, retrieval kind, expected problem/version, benchmark revision,
+ordered objectives and policies. Repeated retrievals return the same evidence
+without execution. A retry may request a different subset of judgment fields.
+Rebinding to different evidence must fail with HTTP 409. Services may store
+retrieval audit records. Yukon handles duplicate solver submissions through
+its existing submission deduplication.
+
+Errors use a non-2xx HTTP status and an optional public `{"error":"..."}` body:
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| 400 | Malformed request or unsupported protocol version |
+| 401 / 403 | Missing service authentication or unauthorized owner |
+| 404 | Unknown receipt/result |
+| 409 | Incomplete result, evidence/policy/objective mismatch or conflicting binding |
+| 429 / 503 | Service temporarily unavailable |
+
+Yukon accepts a judgment only from a valid success envelope. It does not log
+service response bodies or credentials on failures, start a new evaluation, or
+fall back to scores in the receipt. Automatic polling/backoff is not part of v1;
+a failed retrieval can be retried through normal Yukon job handling.
+
+## Adding a project
+
+Implement this endpoint on the verifier, provision account ownership and a
+consumer token, and configure a service alias in Yukon. Import a manual Pareto
+manifest with the appropriate objective order, policies and starter receipt
+while holding its baseline job. Once Yukon assigns the benchmark UUID, an
+operator enables that UUID and the independently reviewed problem/version in
+both services. Then release the baseline job. Never auto-enable a benchmark
+based on uploaded source, a manifest or a receipt. The local launcher leaves a
+new benchmark queued until these explicit entries exist.
+No project-specific Yukon provider code is needed.
+
+Catalyst is one implementation. It interprets its own receipt version,
+evaluation UUID, source/image/problem/pricing digests, checkpoint evidence and
+ECDSA policies. Its adapter returns the standard judgment. Those fields and
+rules are not part of Yukon's shared wire contract.
+
+Catalyst defines the problem ID as `ecdsa.fail` and the version as the SHA-256
+digest of the complete pinned problem-set document used in the evaluation.
+It derives both from the stored run request and checks them against its private
+consumer grant before releasing a score. A correct service key alone cannot
+authorize another benchmark or make a receipt for another problem acceptable.
+
+The provider tests exercise distinct compression and robotics receipts without
+Catalyst fields, and reject mismatched identities, revisions, objectives,
+policies and protocol versions. Deployment and network provisioning are separate
+from protocol conformance.
